INT. DRY-DOCK PUMP HOUSE - NIGHT

A cathedral of valves and rust-orange pipework. A drained dry dock yawns beyond a glass wall, the hull of a half-scrapped ferry propped on blocks. Emergency light only.

KESTREL OYELARAN-FINCH (30s, ex-auditor, wet hair, laptop bag clutched to her chest) backs against a pump housing. In the center of the room, DRAGAN MIHAJLOVIĆ (50s, heavy coat, port authority lanyard) stands with a pistol held low and loose, like a tool he's tired of.

DRAGAN
Put the bag on the floor.

KESTREL
No.

DRAGAN
It's not a negotiation.

KESTREL
It's the only thing keeping you from sitting in a cell.

He tilts his head. A dripping pipe counts seconds.

DRAGAN
The ledgers are digital.

KESTREL
So are the backups.

DRAGAN
Where?

KESTREL
Not telling.

He sighs, and for a moment looks almost sad. Steps forward.

Behind Kestrel, a hatch clangs. Boots on a steel stair. She spins. Dragan doesn't.

A third figure descends: INSPECTOR ROSALIND ACHTERBERG (40s, plain coat, badge on her belt, one hand already on her holster).

ROSALIND
Port Authority Police. Mr. Mihajlović. Lower it.

DRAGAN
Rosalind.

ROSALIND
Lower it, Dragan.

He doesn't. Doesn't raise it either. Kestrel looks from one to the other.

KESTREL
You know each other.

ROSALIND
Twenty years.

KESTREL
Then you can talk him out of this.

ROSALIND
I'm not here to talk him out of anything.

Kestrel's face changes. She takes a step back, into the pump housing.

KESTREL
You're not here to arrest him.

ROSALIND
I'm here to see that the bag leaves with me.

DRAGAN
Ah. There we are.

KESTREL
(quiet)
I called you. You said you'd come.

ROSALIND
And I did.

A fourth voice speaks from the dark above the stair, unamplified, polite.

ALVARO (O.S.)
Everyone relax. Please.

All three look up. A silhouette stands on the catwalk, a phone held loosely to its ear.

DRAGAN
Who is that.

ALVARO (O.S.)
Someone who'd like to hear the rest of this.

ROSALIND
(to Dragan)
You brought a recorder?

DRAGAN
I didn't bring anyone.

ALVARO (O.S.)
No, I came on my own. Mrs. Oyelaran-Finch, I believe that's your bag.

Kestrel stares at the catwalk. Then, slowly, at the two armed people in front of her. She sets the bag on the floor, pushes it with her foot across the concrete toward Rosalind and Dragan, and steps away from it with her hands visible.

KESTREL
It's yours. Whoever wants it.

Neither of them moves. Dragan lowers his pistol an inch. Rosalind's hand leaves her holster. The pipe drips.

ALVARO (O.S.)
(softly)
That's better.

DRAGAN
(muttering)
Twenty years.

ROSALIND
Don't.

She crouches and picks up the bag. Opens it. Looks inside. Closes it.

ROSALIND (CONT'D)
It's empty.

DRAGAN
What?

KESTREL
The laptop's in the dock.

All of them turn to the glass wall. Far below, at the foot of the rusted hull, a tiny blue light glows on the concrete floor of the dry dock, beside a sealed gate that has just begun to let in water.

DRAGAN
You flooded it.

KESTREL
I opened a valve. It's what the pump house is for.

Rosalind sprints for the door. Dragan lowers his gun completely and watches the water, and the blue light, and the woman, one after the other.

ALVARO (O.S.)
(delighted)
Oh, well done.

KESTREL
Don't follow me.

She walks to the stair and climbs past the catwalk without looking at it. At the top, the silhouette steps aside to let her through.
